<?xml version="1.0" encoding="utf-8"?>
<Defs>

  <!--
    THE TOWER FOLK - whoever it is that lives on the friendly floors.

    INHERITS OutlanderFactionBase rather than being written from scratch. That
    parent supplies the four things RimWorld's own error checker demands of any
    humanlike faction - backstoryFilters, maxPawnCostPerTotalPointsCurve,
    raidLootValueFromPointsCurve and a pawnGroupMakers list that is actually
    populated - and getting them by inheritance means they stay correct when
    the base game changes them.

    HIDDEN, and that is the whole point. Faction.HasGoodwill returns false for a
    hidden faction, so TryAffectGoodwillWith is a no-op in both directions:
    trading here buys no free goodwill, and massacring a market floor costs the
    player nothing in their real world relations. It also keeps them out of the
    Factions tab, which is right - these people are not a power on the planet,
    they are somebody's neighbours inside a tower.

    Explicitly NOT the Adventurers' Guild faction. That one is visible with real
    goodwill, so tower violence would leak outward in a way the player could not
    see coming.

    requiredCountAtGameStart 0 with canMakeRandomly false: the faction is created
    at runtime by TowerVillage.EnsureFaction the first time a village floor
    generates, so mid-save installs work and worlds without a tower never carry
    a faction they have no use for.
  -->
  <FactionDef ParentName="OutlanderFactionBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_TowerFolk</defName>
    <label>tower folk</label>
    <description>People who live inside the tower, on the floors that turned out to be worth living on. They did not climb here and they are not trying to leave. They will trade with anyone who is not currently trying to kill them.</description>
    <pawnSingular>tower dweller</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>tower dwellers</pawnsPlural>
    <hidden>true</hidden>
    <humanlikeFaction>true</humanlikeFaction>
    <canMakeRandomly>false</canMakeRandomly>
    <requiredCountAtGameStart>0</requiredCountAtGameStart>
    <maxCountAtGameStart>0</maxCountAtGameStart>
    <!-- No presence on the planet: the tower is the only place they exist. -->
    <settlementGenerationWeight>0</settlementGenerationWeight>
    <canSiege>false</canSiege>
    <canStageAttacks>false</canStageAttacks>
    <raidsForbidden>true</raidsForbidden>
    <canGenerateQuestSites>false</canGenerateQuestSites>
    <permanentEnemy>false</permanentEnemy>
    <leaderTitle>elder</leaderTitle>
    <techLevel>Medieval</techLevel>
    <colorSpectrum>
      <li>(0.85, 0.78, 0.60)</li>
      <li>(0.70, 0.66, 0.55)</li>
    </colorSpectrum>
  </FactionDef>


  <!--
    THE MIRROR FACTION - the tower's blank hand, for enemies who are supposed
    to be NOBODY'S: floor 50's copies of the player's own party, and any
    future authored tower enemy that must carry no faction identity at all.

    It exists because "no faction" is not actually available. A hostile
    humanlike needs SOME faction for lords and hostility, and resolving "the
    first permanently hostile faction" handed mirrors to whatever the modlist
    provided - whose xenotypeSet then drove pawn generation. That is how a
    baseliner colonist's copy generated with another faction's Yttakin
    germline before its genes were overwritten. This faction pins generation
    to baseliner (the gene copy then rebuilds the real xenotype verbatim), and
    its apparel/ideo influence is irrelevant because the character spec clears
    and rewrites all of it.

    Same lifecycle as the tower folk above: hidden, no planet presence,
    created at runtime (MirrorForge.EnsureMirrorFaction) the first time it is
    needed. permanentEnemy where the folk are neutral - a mirror exists to be
    fought, and goodwill must never leak from killing one.
  -->
  <FactionDef ParentName="OutlanderFactionBase">
    <defName>IsekaiDungeons_MirrorFaction</defName>
    <label>the tower's hand</label>
    <description>The tower does not need to recruit. When it wants soldiers, it makes them - out of whatever, and whoever, is available.</description>
    <pawnSingular>reflection</pawnSingular>
    <pawnsPlural>reflections</pawnsPlural>
    <hidden>true</hidden>
    <humanlikeFaction>true</humanlikeFaction>
    <canMakeRandomly>false</canMakeRandomly>
    <requiredCountAtGameStart>0</requiredCountAtGameStart>
    <maxCountAtGameStart>0</maxCountAtGameStart>
    <settlementGenerationWeight>0</settlementGenerationWeight>
    <canSiege>false</canSiege>
    <canStageAttacks>false</canStageAttacks>
    <raidsForbidden>true</raidsForbidden>
    <canGenerateQuestSites>false</canGenerateQuestSites>
    <permanentEnemy>true</permanentEnemy>
    <leaderTitle>hand</leaderTitle>
    <techLevel>Spacer</techLevel>
    <!-- Generation-neutral: an EMPTY chance list is vanilla's way of saying
         "always baseliner" (Baseliner is the implicit remainder and is never
         listed as an entry). Inherit False wipes whatever spread the
         outlander base carries, so nothing here can ever bleed into a pawn
         whose real identity is applied on top. -->
    <xenotypeSet Inherit="False">
      <xenotypeChances />
    </xenotypeSet>
    <colorSpectrum>
      <li>(0.55, 0.55, 0.62)</li>
      <li>(0.35, 0.35, 0.42)</li>
    </colorSpectrum>
  </FactionDef>

</Defs>
